\description{
Approximates the posterior of a parameter vector from its log density
alone, using the approximation pipeline of INLAvaan (Jamil & Rue, 2026a,
2026b) with no latent field and nothing model-specific. The pipeline has
four stages:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Joint Gaussian (Laplace).} The posterior mode \eqn{\vartheta^*} and
the inverse Hessian \eqn{\Omega} there give a Gaussian approximation
\eqn{N(\vartheta^*, \Omega)} and the Laplace estimate of the log marginal
likelihood.
\item \strong{VB mean correction.} The Gaussian is recentred at
\eqn{\tilde\vartheta = \vartheta^* + \hat\delta}, the mean that maximises
its expected log-posterior, with the covariance unchanged.
\item \strong{Skew-normal marginals.} For each parameter, the log-posterior is
scanned along the conditional mode path of the Gaussian, corrected for the
changing volume of the other parameters, and fitted by a skew-normal
\eqn{SN(\hat\xi_j, \hat\omega_j, \hat\alpha_j)}.
\item \strong{Gaussian copula draws.} \code{\link[=sampling]{sampling()}} couples the skew-normal marginals
through the correlations of \eqn{\Omega}.
}

Stages 2 and 3 are optional and independent of each other.
\code{vb_correction = "none"} skips the VB correction, which leaves the marginals
unchanged (they are scanned around the mode) but gives the Laplace log
marginal likelihood. \code{marginals = "gaussian"} skips the scans and uses the
Gaussian's own marginals \eqn{N(\tilde\vartheta_j, \Omega_{jj})}, centred at
the VB mean (or at the mode when \code{vb_correction = "none"}); \code{\link[=sampling]{sampling()}} then
draws from the joint Gaussian. So
\verb{vb_correction = "none", marginals = "gaussian"} is the Laplace approximation
alone, and \code{marginals = "gaussian"} alone is the Laplace approximation with
the VB correction.
}
\section{Writing \code{logpost}}{

The approximation works on an unconstrained scale: every parameter must be
free to take any real value. Reparameterise anything constrained, for
example a standard deviation \eqn{\sigma > 0} as \eqn{\log\sigma}, a
probability as its logit, or a correlation as its Fisher \eqn{z}. Means and
regression coefficients need no change.

Return \code{-Inf} wherever the density is zero or cannot be evaluated, such as
outside the support or where a matrix is not positive definite. \code{NA} and
\code{NaN} count the same, and so does any value at or below \eqn{-10^{39}}{-1e39},
such as the \code{-1e40} that some code, lavaan's likelihoods among it, returns
where it fails. The scans and the VB nodes reach several posterior SDs from
the mode, and an error raised there stops the fit.

\code{logpost} is then the log density \strong{of the parameters you pass}. When a
prior is stated on the natural scale, include the log-Jacobian of the
transformation: with \eqn{\vartheta = \log\sigma} and a prior
\eqn{p(\sigma)}, the prior term is \eqn{\log p(e^\vartheta) + \vartheta}.
Leaving it out targets a different posterior, not a worse approximation of
the same one, and nothing downstream can detect it.

The choice of parameterisation also sets the quality of the approximation,
since each parameter gets its own skew-normal on the scale you choose.
Scales on which the posterior is close to Gaussian (log standard
deviations, Fisher-\eqn{z} correlations, logits) work best. A parameter
named for its scale is reported on the natural one (see the next section),
and other derived quantities are available through
\code{sampling(fit, fun = ...)}.

The log marginal likelihood is an estimate of \eqn{\log p(y)} only when
\code{logpost} returns the full \eqn{\log p(y, \vartheta)}, including every
normalising constant of the likelihood and the prior. Otherwise it is off by
the missing constants.
}

\section{Natural-scale summaries}{

Name a parameter for the scale it is on and the results are reported on
its natural scale. The name is a prefix from the table, an underscore, and
the name of the natural-scale parameter:\tabular{lll}{
   Name in \code{init} \tab \code{logpost} receives \tab Reported as \cr
   \code{log_sigma} \tab \eqn{\log\sigma}{log(sigma)} \tab \code{sigma}, \eqn{e^\vartheta}{exp(theta)} \cr
   \code{logit_p} \tab \eqn{\log\{p / (1 - p)\}}{log(p / (1 - p))} \tab \code{p}, \eqn{1 / (1 + e^{-\vartheta})}{plogis(theta)} \cr
   \code{atanh_rho} \tab \eqn{\operatorname{artanh}\rho}{atanh(rho)} \tab \code{rho}, \eqn{\tanh\vartheta}{tanh(theta)} \cr
}


The log suits a positive parameter (an SD, a variance, a precision, a
rate or a shape), the logit one between 0 and 1 (a probability), and the
inverse hyperbolic tangent, Fisher's \eqn{z}, one between -1 and 1 (a
correlation).
These are the scales R-INLA itself works on, for nearly all of its
hyperparameters: the log, the logit, and for a correlation
\eqn{\log\{(1 + \rho) / (1 - \rho)\}}{log((1 + rho) / (1 - rho))}, which
is twice Fisher's \eqn{z}.

Nothing changes in the fit. \code{logpost}, \code{grad}, \code{hess}, the \code{loglik} of
\code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} and the \code{fun} of \code{\link[=sampling]{sampling()}} all receive the parameter under the
name and on the scale of \code{init}, and \code{logpost} must still include the
log-Jacobian of a prior stated on the natural scale. Only the results are
mapped back, each from its own skew-normal marginal: \code{\link[=print]{print()}},
\code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}, \code{\link[=coef]{coef()}} and \code{\link[=plot]{plot()}} show \code{sigma}, and \code{\link[=sampling]{sampling()}} returns
draws of it. The quantiles are the fitted quantiles mapped through the
transform. The mean, SD and mode are those of the natural-scale
distribution, so the mean of \code{sigma} is not the exponential of the mean of
\code{log_sigma}. A line under the table says which rows were mapped, and
\code{fit$transform} lists them. The diagnostics stay on the scale of the
computation, under the names in \code{init}.

A prefix counts only when an underscore and a name follow it, in lower
case, so \code{logistic}, \code{log_} and \code{Log_sigma} are left alone. A name that
would clash, such as \code{log_sigma} beside a parameter called \code{sigma}, is
left on its own scale with a warning. Only these one-to-one maps of a
single parameter are recognised: a quantity that depends on several
parameters, such as a covariance, comes from \code{sampling(fit, fun = ...)}.
\code{transform = FALSE} reports every parameter as \code{logpost} receives it, and
\code{summary()}, \code{plot()} and \code{sampling()} take the same argument for a fit
already made.
}

\section{Accuracy}{

The skew-normal marginals capture skewness that the Laplace approximation
misses, and they are accurate to the extent that a skew-normal fits each
marginal (see the diagnostics in \code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}}). Like INLAvaan,
each marginal is scanned along a straight line through the mode, and the
changing volume of the other parameters enters only to first order. When a
parameter's spread depends strongly on another, poorly determined
parameter, its marginal comes out too narrow, by a factor that vanishes as
O(1/n). Examples are a regression coefficient or a mean when the noise SD
is uncertain. For a normal mean with unknown variance the SD is about 11\%
too small at n = 12, 3\% at n = 50, and under 1\% at n = 200. The
\code{ridge_bend} diagnostic of \code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}} flags this narrowing
where it predicts about 10\% or more. By default
(\code{marginal_scan = "linear"}) this is the scan of INLAvaan;
\code{marginal_scan = "laplace"}, or \code{tk_fallback = TRUE} for the flagged
parameters only, removes the narrowing at a higher cost (see the section on
the scans).

A skew-normal also cannot follow a tail heavier than a Gaussian's. In a
structural equation model with 39 parameters and n = 75, the data could not
keep two residual variances away from zero, so on the log scale their
posteriors had a long, exponential left tail, set by the prior. Checked
against MCMC, those two skew-normal marginals were less than half as wide
as the posterior, with the 2.5\% quantile about 2 posterior SDs too high;
the other 37 were within 7\% in SD. On the variance scale, where the tail
near zero is compressed, the two were much closer: means 0.2 posterior SDs
too high and SDs 10 to 15\% too small. INLAvaan's marginals of this model
are the same as inlavanilla's: Jamil and Rue (2026a, Section 4.4) find
the same miss in the lower tail of a variance near zero, the boundary
pile-up that a unimodal skew-normal cannot capture. No check is aimed at
this. The \code{ridge_bend} diagnostic of \code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}} flagged one
of the two (0.44), but the other's ridge bend (0.11) was no larger than
that of marginals within 7\% in SD, and the \code{tails} check, which looks only
4 posterior SDs either side of the mode (the Laplace SDs), missed both.
}

\section{The scans}{

Each skew-normal scan follows the conditional mode path (CMP) of the
Laplace Gaussian, \eqn{\vartheta(t) = \vartheta^* + t v_j} with
\eqn{v_j = \Omega_{\cdot j} / \sqrt{\Omega_{jj}}}{v_j = Omega[, j] /
sqrt(Omega[j, j])}, so that \eqn{t} counts posterior SDs. The log marginal
is then about the height of the log-posterior along the path plus the
width term \eqn{\gamma_j(t) = -\frac12 \log|H_{-j}(t)|}{gamma_j(t) =
-1/2 log|H_-j(t)|}, where \eqn{H_{-j}}{H_-j} is the negative Hessian of
the log-posterior with row and column j deleted: the log-volume of the
other parameters. \code{marginal_scan} sets how the scan is taken, in four
levels that each remove one error:
\itemize{
\item \code{"plain"} leaves the width term out, and fits the heights alone.
\item \code{"linear"}, the default and INLAvaan's, linearises the width term,
\eqn{\gamma_j(t) \approx \gamma_j' t}{gamma_j(t) ~ gamma_j' t}, with
the slope from whitened differences of the gradient (Jamil & Rue, 2026a,
Corollary 3.3). Its difference from \code{"plain"} is the volume correction.
\item \code{"exact"} takes \eqn{\gamma_j(t)}{gamma_j(t)} exactly along the same
path. Its difference from \code{"linear"} is the error of the linearisation.
\item \code{"laplace"} moves the other parameters to their conditional mode at each
point of the scan and takes the height and the width term there: the
Laplace approximation of the marginal (Tierney & Kadane, 1986), INLA's
"Laplace strategy". Its difference from \code{"exact"} is the error of the
straight path, which the \code{ridge_bend} diagnostic of
\code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}} predicts.
}

The skew-normal is fitted to the corrected heights in the same way for
all four, on the same grid. \code{tk_fallback = TRUE} keeps the cheap scan
for every parameter but those whose ridge bend is flagged, and rescans
those with \code{"laplace"}. In \code{fit$diagnostics$marginals}, \code{marginal_scan}
gives the scan each parameter used and \code{gamma1} the slope of its width term
at the mode (for \code{"exact"} and \code{"laplace"}, a central difference across the
mode, and \code{NA} for \code{"plain"}). The ridge bend of a parameter scanned with
\code{"laplace"} is still reported, but not flagged.
}

\section{Gradients and Hessians}{

\code{grad} is optional. Without it, derivatives come from finite differences
of \code{logpost}, and the number of calls grows with the square of the number
of parameters m. The mode and Hessian take about m^2 + 6m calls, and the
marginals about m(\code{n_grid} + 2m + 6), with 2(m - 1) more for each parameter
whose ridge bend is above 0.1. The VB correction takes 2(m + 1)(m + 2) + 8m
calls, or (m + 1)(m + 2) per sweep with \code{vb_correction = "full"}: it needs
no derivatives, because Stein's identity gives the expected gradient from
values of \code{logpost} alone. With \code{vb_correction = "full"}, the VB mean is then
exact
when the log-posterior is a polynomial of degree four in whitened
coordinates, and up to degree six with \code{grad}. On test models it was about
0.01 posterior SDs from exact VB (0.003 with \code{grad}), and up to 0.1 on a
strongly skewed posterior (0.03 with \code{grad}), against a VB error of 0.01 to
0.2 SDs from the posterior mean itself. On the most skewed posteriors the VB
correction of \code{vb_correction = "full"} may then not settle, and the fit says
it has not converged (see \code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}}); \code{grad} is the remedy.

With \code{grad}, every stage is more accurate. The fit is also faster when a
gradient call costs about as much as a \code{logpost} call, as a hand-written
gradient usually does, since it then makes far fewer calls in all. A
gradient by automatic differentiation (RTMB, TMB) costs about two, and
beyond a handful of parameters the fit is still several times faster than
without one (four times at m = 39). The default VB correction,
\code{vb_correction = "onestep"}, takes 2m + 1 gradient calls, about as many as
one numerical Hessian, and \code{vb_correction = "full"} takes at most (m + 1)(m +
2) + 1 per sweep, usually about four sweeps. Beyond a handful of parameters
the marginals are the slowest stage, along with the VB correction under
\code{vb_correction = "full"}. \code{vb_correction = "none"} skips the VB correction
(and its term in the log marginal likelihood), and the skew-normal marginals
do not depend on it; \code{marginals = "gaussian"} saves the calls of the scans.

\code{hess} is optional too, and needs \code{grad}, as in \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}}. With
it, \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}} takes Newton steps to the mode, and the Hessian at
the mode is the supplied one. The VB correction does not use it, nor do the
default marginals, whose volume correction takes 4m + 1 \code{grad} calls for all
the parameters together, where a Hessian each would cost far more.
\code{marginal_scan = "exact"} and \code{"laplace"} do use it. The fit is the one
\code{grad} alone gives, to the error of its finite differences (about 1e-8,
relative), except that the mode is found more precisely. A dense Hessian
costs about m gradients, so this takes a little longer: a structural equation
model with 39 parameters and an automatic-differentiation Hessian (RTMB) took
0.18 s with it and 0.17 s without.

The fit goes straight to the mode: at \code{init} it only checks that \code{logpost},
\code{grad} and \code{hess} return finite values of the right shape. A supplied \code{grad}
is compared with \code{logpost} at the mode, and the Hessian there, whether taken
from \code{grad} or supplied, is checked for symmetry, with a warning if either
check fails (see \code{grad_gap} in \code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}}). Without \code{hess}, the
comparison reads differences the fit takes there anyway to set its steps, at
no cost in calls. With \code{hess} it takes them for the check alone: 2 \code{logpost}
calls per try of the step search, usually 3 or 4 tries per parameter, so
about 6m to 8m calls, and up to 60m when the search fails. A supplied \code{hess}
is also checked at the mode against central differences of \code{grad}, along two
directions, with a warning if it does not match, and its diagonal is part of
what \code{grad_gap} compares with \code{logpost}. Any R function works for \code{logpost},
\code{grad} and \code{hess}, including compiled code (Rcpp, TMB) and RTMB, which
differentiates R code. An RTMB function can return the log-posterior itself,
and its \code{obj$fn}, \code{obj$gr} and \code{obj$he} are then passed as they are. For a
TMB template that returns the negative log-posterior, pass
\code{function(x) -obj$fn(x)}, \code{function(x) -as.vector(obj$gr(x))} and
\code{function(x) -obj$he(x)}, with \code{init = obj$par}; its repeated names (one per
element of a parameter vector) are numbered, as \code{beta[1]}, \code{beta[2]}, and so
on. TMB gives no Hessian for a model with random effects.
}

\section{The VB correction}{

The VB mean correction moves the centre of the Laplace Gaussian towards the
posterior, keeping its covariance. It maximises the expected log-posterior
under that Gaussian, so that at the solution the gradient, averaged over the
Gaussian, is zero.

\code{vb_correction = "onestep"} takes the first Newton step towards that
solution, with the Laplace curvature, and stops. With a gradient, the average
is taken over the mode and \eqn{\pm\sqrt 3}{+/- sqrt(3)} along each axis of
the symmetric square root of the covariance, taken on the correlation scale.
Without one, it is one pass of Stein's identity on the rule of
\code{vb_correction = "full"}. Most of the movement is in that step, and exact VB
is itself an approximation to the posterior mean with an error of the same
order.

One step is the first iterate of a fixed-point map, and it approximates the
solution only where that map is a contraction. The fit checks this, with a
second sweep at the step. The step must reduce the residual of the VB
equation (\code{vb_lhat} in \code{diagnostics()}, the ratio of the residual at the step
to the one at the mode, below 1), and the slope of the map along the step
must lie between -1 and 1 at the mode and at the step (\code{vb_j0} and \code{vb_j1}).
When the check passes, the one step is the correction. When it fails, the
posterior is far from Gaussian along the step, and the fit solves the VB
equation instead, on the same sweeps and from the mode, by Broyden's method.
It then raises a warning that names the parameter the one step moved
furthest. The warning comes whether or not the solve converged, since it is
about the posterior and not the solver: treat the posterior means, the log
marginal likelihood, \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} and \code{\link[=waic]{waic()}} of such a fit with care, and
compare with MCMC if they matter. \code{vb_solve} in \code{diagnostics()} says whether
the solve ran, and \code{vb_step_sigma} is then the step still to go where it
stopped, which is recorded and not flagged. If \code{logpost} or \code{grad} is not
finite at a node around the mode there is no step to check, and the
optimiser of \code{vb_correction = "full"} takes over.

The check, the solve, the warning and the diagnostics are the same with and
without a gradient. Only the sweep differs. With a gradient it is 2m + 1
gradients, so the correction of a fit that passes takes 4m + 2 gradient
calls. Without one it is a Stein pass of (m + 1)(m + 2) values of \code{logpost},
and the two slopes take 4m values each. The sweeps of a correction, the two
of the check among them, are capped at twice the number of calls that the
mode and Hessian took, divided by 2m + 1. A fit that passes the check takes
its two sweeps and never meets the cap. With a gradient the correction of a
fit that fails it therefore costs no more than twice what the mode and
Hessian did. Without one the cap is the same number of sweeps, each of which
costs more: a fit that fails the check can then spend up to
2(m + 1)(m + 2) / (2m + 1) times the calls of its mode and Hessian, about m
times, on the correction. That is the price of a fit without a gradient, and
\code{grad} is the remedy. The factor of two is \code{vb_budget} in
\code{\link[=vanilla_control]{vanilla_control()}}, and \code{Inf} there removes the cap.

A large first step can also overstate the VB term of the log marginal
likelihood, so after a shift above 1 posterior SD, which is flagged, compare
models with care: \code{vb_correction = "full"} may be closer.

\code{vb_correction = "full"} iterates to the solution, on a degree-5 rule with at
most (m + 1)(m + 2) + 1 nodes, and hands over to \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}} if it does
not converge.
}

\section{Parallel computation}{

Set \code{cores} to run the per-parameter scans of the skew-normal marginals in
parallel:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{cores <- parallelly::availableCores(omit = 1)
fit <- inla_vanilla(logpost, init, marginal_scan = "laplace", cores = cores)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{parallelly::availableCores()} counts the cores the machine offers, and
\code{omit = 1} leaves one free. A fit never asks for more workers than the
machine offers. The results and the progress line are those of a serial fit.
A long scan uses all the \code{cores}, a shorter one fewer, and a scan expected to
take under a second stays serial. The mode, the Hessian and the VB correction
stay serial, so the gain is largest with \code{marginal_scan = "laplace"} or
\code{"exact"}, where the scans are nearly the whole fit: about 7.5 times faster
on 12 cores on test models. With the default scan the scans are about half of
a slow fit, and it was about 1.5 times at most.

\code{cores} needs the future package, which is optional. It uses forked workers
where those are safe, in a terminal on macOS or Linux, and separate R
sessions elsewhere: on Windows, in RStudio, and in Positron's console, which
refuses to fork. Forks share everything. Separate sessions need the package
installed, not just loaded with \code{devtools::load_all()}, and are sent your
functions and data. Compiled code (Rcpp, TMB, RTMB) cannot be sent, so the
fit first checks that a worker gets the same value at the mode, and
otherwise runs serially with a message.

To use separate sessions with compiled code, rebuild it on each worker: put
whatever creates it in a function, and call that function again when the
process is not the one that started the fit. With RTMB and random effects
(without them, RTMB needs none of this):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{build <- function() \{
  RTMB::MakeADFun(model, parameters, random = "u", silent = TRUE)
\}
obj <- build()
pid <- Sys.getpid()
rebuild <- function() \{
  if (Sys.getpid() != pid) \{
    obj <<- build()
    pid <<- Sys.getpid()
  \}
\}
logpost <- function(theta) \{
  rebuild()
  -obj$fn(theta)
\}
grad <- function(theta) \{
  rebuild()
  -obj$gr(theta)
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With Rcpp, \code{rebuild()} calls \code{Rcpp::sourceCpp(file, cacheDir = dir)} instead,
with a \code{dir} of your own so that the workers share one compiled copy. The
RTMB and Rcpp articles on the package website have worked versions.

With \code{cores} left at \code{NULL} the fit follows a plan set with \code{future::plan()},
and runs serially if there is none, so a plan can apply to every fit in a
session. \code{cores = 1} runs serially whatever the plan. \code{\link[=loo]{loo()}} and \code{\link[=waic]{waic()}}
use the setting of the fit.
}

\section{Output}{

By default each stage prints one line as it finishes, with the time it
took, and shows a progress bar while it runs for longer than half a
second, or than the option \code{cli.progress_show_after} when it is set. In
knitr or Quarto documents, where a bar cannot redraw in place, a larger
\code{cli.progress_show_after} in the setup chunk keeps bars out of the output.
Warnings, such as a gradient that does not match \code{logpost}, are signalled
as usual. A fit whose checks look off, such as a mode that was not
reached, ends with one warning that lists them, as INLAvaan's does (see
\code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}} and \code{\link[=diagnostics]{diagnostics()}}).

\code{verbose = TRUE} adds a full diagnostic of the fit, in grey lines under
each stage. It reports what the fit computes anyway, with no further calls
of \code{logpost} or \code{grad}:
\itemize{
\item Before the mode search: the parameters, where the derivatives come from,
and \code{logpost} at \code{init}.
\item The mode: the message, iterations and evaluations of \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}},
and whether it was restarted with the parameters rescaled (the
iterations and evaluations are then the totals over both runs, and the
restart line gives each run's own iterations); the
log-posterior at the mode, the whitened gradient there (which decides
the restart) and the largest Newton step in posterior SDs (which the
fit-time warning checks); how the Hessian was taken (and, for a
supplied \code{grad}, the asymmetry of the Hessian and its gap from
\code{logpost}, and, for a supplied \code{hess}, how far it is from central
differences of \code{grad}); the largest posterior
correlation and the smallest eigenvalue of the correlation matrix, which
is near zero when some combination of the parameters is poorly
identified, and the condition number of the Hessian in posterior-SD
units; and the Laplace log marginal likelihood.
\item The VB correction: the largest step, in posterior SDs, at each
fixed-point iteration, whether Anderson acceleration or the fallback
optimiser was used (and, after the fallback, the step still to go where
it landed, and whether it was rerun from there), the largest shift, and
the term it adds to the log marginal likelihood.
\item The marginals: the shape, NMAD, ridge bend, tails, unscanned mass and
volume correction \code{gamma1} of each parameter, with the values that
\code{\link[=summary.inla_vanilla]{summary.inla_vanilla()}} flags marked.
\item The calls of \code{logpost}, \code{grad} and \code{hess} in each stage, and in all,
with the total time.
}

For the iteration trace of \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}} itself, pass
\code{control = vanilla_control(nlminb = list(trace = 1))}.

\code{silent = TRUE} prints nothing, for simulations and other repeated fits:
no progress bars and no stage lines, and every warning and message raised
during the fit, including those of \code{logpost}, \code{grad} and \code{hess}, is
muffled. Errors are still raised. Output printed with \code{\link[=cat]{cat()}} or
\code{\link[=print]{print()}}, by \code{logpost}, \code{grad}, \code{hess} or the trace of \code{\link[stats:nlminb]{stats::nlminb()}},
is not captured.

Whatever the level, the warnings raised during the fit are recorded in
\code{fit$warnings}, so that a simulation can check them afterwards.
}

\examples{
# Normal data with unknown mean and standard deviation, on the scale
# (mu, log sigma)
set.seed(1)
y <- rnorm(30, mean = 5, sd = 2)

logpost <- function(theta) {
  mu <- theta[["mu"]]
  sigma <- exp(theta[["log_sigma"]])
  sum(dnorm(y, mu, sigma, log = TRUE)) +
    dnorm(mu, 0, 10, log = TRUE) + # prior on mu
    dexp(sigma, rate = 1, log = TRUE) + # prior on sigma ...
    theta[["log_sigma"]] # ... and its Jacobian
}

# The parameter named log_sigma is reported as sigma
fit <- inla_vanilla(logpost, init = c(mu = 0, log_sigma = 0))
fit

# The same fit as logpost sees it
summary(fit, transform = FALSE)$table

# A quantity of several parameters, through the draws
cv <- sampling(fit, fun = function(theta) {
  exp(theta[["log_sigma"]]) / theta[["mu"]]
})
quantile(cv, c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))

# A full diagnostic of each stage
fit <- inla_vanilla(logpost, init = c(mu = 0, log_sigma = 0), verbose = TRUE)

# Nothing printed, as in a simulation; any warnings are kept in the fit
fit <- inla_vanilla(logpost, init = c(mu = 0, log_sigma = 0), silent = TRUE)
fit$warnings
}
